#[allow(clippy::too_many_lines)]
pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── B+: per-slice decay factors in a gated linear-attention
        // recurrence are exp of the DIFFERENCE of accumulated log gates —
        // non-positive by construction, so no clamp ever; the cheaper
        // mid-chunk-reference factoring creates positive exponents and is
        // REFUSED on the paper's measured numerics (the anti-arm IS the
        // measured-negative-ledger content).
        // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "log-gate-diff-decay-no-clamp-stability".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (log-gate-diff decay, no clamp by construction): in a gated linear-attention recurrence the per-slice decay factor between two tokens is evaluated as exp of the DIFFERENCE of accumulated log gates — decay(r <- s) = exp(gc[r] - gc[s]) where gc is the chunk-local cumulative sum of the per-token log gate g (g <= 0). The argument is non-positive BY CONSTRUCTION: a cumsum of non-positive terms is non-increasing, so any within-chunk r >= s difference is <= 0, exp lands in (0, 1], and the exponentiation can NEVER overflow — no clamp, no cap, ever. The tempting CHEAPER refactor — factoring every decay around a mid-chunk reference point (2*C*E exponentials instead of C*C*E) — breaks the construction: a pair whose target sits before the reference gets a POSITIVE half-exponent (gc[r] - gc[mid] > 0), which overflows FP32 above e^88.7; clamping THAT exponent keeps the run alive but no longer telescopes to the true decay — the paper's FP64 emulation of just that clamp measures rel. error 0.05 at within-chunk decay e^-200 and 1.5 at e^-600 — so the cheap form is REFUSED on measured numerics, not taste. Logs accumulate; only DIFFERENCES are exponentiated; the extra exponentials are the price of never overflowing. THE SOURCE LAW, verbatim at the pin (OliverSieberling/cute-triadic-gdn @ 2caa4098073da92c2ba0d573df6453ceb7dbce45): the stability sentence, ops/gdn_joint.py:24-25 — 'Every exponential the kernels take has argument <= 0 (the chunk-local cumsum of a non-positive log gate is non-positive), so the decay span needs no cap.'; the gate's sign is an input contract, ops/gdn_joint.py:17 — 'log of the decay gate of each slice, <= 0'; the log-space accumulation is a kernel of its own, kernels/sm90_joint_scan.py:1 — 'Chunk-local cumulative log decay (log gamma of the paper) with two 32-token parallel prefix sums per chunk.'; the accumulation itself, ops/gdn_joint.py:36-39 `_cumsum` — 'chunk-local inclusive cumsum over T, (B,T,H,E) -> (B,T,H,E)', body `g.view(B, T // CHUNK, CHUNK, H, E).cumsum(2).view(B, T, H, E)`. Paper-side (App. A via riir-infer .research/007:30): 'Every decay factor `γ^r_e/γ^s_e` is evaluated directly as `exp(diff of accumulated log gates)`; the difference is non-positive by construction, so **no clamp is ever needed**. The cheaper construction (factor around a mid-chunk reference, 2CE exponentials instead of C²E) creates positive exponents that overflow FP32 above e^88.7; their FP64 emulation of just that clamp measures rel. error 0.05 at within-chunk decay e⁻²⁰⁰ and 1.5 at e⁻⁶⁰⁰ — the cheap form is REFUSED on measured numerics, not taste.' TWO SOURCE-ADJACENT NUANCES, both verified at the pin so the law is neither over- nor under-read: (1) the only clamp near the decay path is a MASK, not a stability cap — the torch reference's pairwise decay, ops/gdn_joint.py:54, is `(gcc[..., :, None, :] - gcc[..., None, :, :]).clamp(max=0.0).exp()`, forcing FUTURE positions (t < s) to decay 1 before the strict-lower masking at :59 (`(bc[..., None] * P * M).tril(-1)`) discards them — causality masking, never an overflow guard; (2) a reference-point form IS admissible where the reference choice PRESERVES the bound: the same torch reference carries the state across the chunk boundary anchored at the chunk END, ops/gdn_joint.py:87-89 (`gL = gcc[:, -1]` then `wc * (gL[:, None] - gcc).exp()`), and since the cumsum is non-increasing the end is its minimum, so gL - gc[t] <= 0 for every t — end-anchored decay-to-boundary keeps every exponent non-positive, while the pairwise matrix itself stays direct-difference (:54) and the MID reference is what breaks the bound. exp of a bare accumulated log is the same argument (:79, `wc * gcc.exp()`) — in (0, 1]. DECISION CONTENT: evaluate decay factors as exp of differences of ACCUMULATED LOGS when the per-step log quantities are sign-bounded (a decay gate g <= 0 makes every cumsum difference non-positive) — the argument bound is a CONSTRUCTION, not a clamp; REFUSE the mid-reference factorization (or ANY refactor that can produce a positive exponent) even when it counts fewer exponentials — its overflow is measured (FP64-emulated clamp rel-err 0.05 @ e^-200, 1.5 @ e^-600, paper App. A via riir-infer .research/007:30) and clamping the overflow loses the long-decay span the recurrence exists to model; a reference form is admissible only where the reference choice provably preserves the non-positive bound (the chunk END qualifies, a mid point does not); when a bound cannot be proven by construction (arbitrary-sign increments), fall back to sign-branch evaluation, never a clamp. REGIME: gated linear-attention / decay-based recurrences (GDN, DeltaNet, Mamba-class gates) evaluating cross-token decay factors. ANTI-REGIME: increments of arbitrary sign (no construction bound — use sign-branch forms); factors needed in EXP form for further multiplication chains are still fine (exp of non-positive is in (0, 1] and products of such stay bounded); a pointwise nonlinearity with no accumulated state (that is the sign-branch rule's face, not this one). NUMBERS HONESTY: e^88.7 / 0.05 @ e^-200 / 1.5 @ e^-600 are PAPER-side figures (arXiv:2609.36529 App. A, FP64 emulation) — carried with the provenance label '(paper App. A via riir-infer .research/007:30)' and never adoption bars; no repo-side timing number exists anywhere in the quotes (the two 32-token prefix sums per chunk are the source's structure, not a timing). NotBenchable here (no sm90/sm100 cuTe host in the corpus — our CUDA lane is sm89, our M3 lane is Metal/CubeCL). KINSHIP (do not double-count): sign-branch-stable-sigmoid (category B, entries_gpu_03) sign-branches ONE sigmoid so exp's ARGUMENT stays <= 0 pointwise — THIS accumulates logs so every DIFFERENCE is non-positive by construction, no branch needed; softplus-piecewise-saturation-clamp (entries_gpu_02) clamps the OUTPUT in the far tail — THIS never clamps anything; wkv-logsumexp-three-channel-state (category G, entries_gpu_38) is a different mechanism — logsumexp state channels, not decay-factor evaluation; cumsum-gate-to-decay-matrix (entries_popcorn) owns the FORM this rule makes safe — 'convert per-step log-gate into pairwise decay matrix via cumsum + diff' is the stable construction as a speedup with no stability law attached, THIS owns the NUMERICS LAW (why the differences' sign is a construction bound, why no clamp, why the cheaper mid-reference variant is refused) — compose, never merge. Measured-negative-ledger kinship (the fused-serving-stack-measured-negative-ledger class, entries_gpu_57 — rejected levers recorded with measured reasons so a future fork does not re-run them): THIS rule's anti-arm IS the ledger content — the mid-chunk-reference factorization is the paper's measured negative, carried in-rule so nobody re-tries the cheap form blind. Corpus greps (grep -noE over src/domains/kernel_opt/corpus/): 'log gate' (spaced) — zero hits; 'log-gate' — entries_gpu_65.rs:94 (chain-rule-postprocess-kernel: the gradient-SPLIT sense, the dgamma-to-dg post-kernel), entries_popcorn.rs:991,997,999 (cumsum-gate-to-decay-matrix, the kin above) + tests.rs:623 (its coverage assert); 'decay span' — zero hits; 'no clamp' — entries_gpu_07.rs:212 (pre-gate-recovery-by-division-cancels-algebraically: the algebraic-elimination sense) + entries_gpu_18.rs:103,105 (lane-sequenced-deterministic-bucket-scatter: the clamp-OFF default sense), no decay owner; 'accumulated log' — zero hits; '88.7' — entries_gpu_03.rs:458,459 (sign-branch-stable-sigmoid's pointwise-argument anchor) + mod.rs:487 + tests.rs:2005 (its coverage assert), all the sigmoid face, no decay-difference owner. CITE: OliverSieberling/cute-triadic-gdn @ 2caa4098073da92c2ba0d573df6453ceb7dbce45 -- cute_triadic_gdn/ops/gdn_joint.py:17 (the g <= 0 input contract) + :24-25 (the stability sentence) + :36-39 (_cumsum) + :54+:59 (the masking clamp and tril) + :79 + :87-89 (the end-anchored reference form) + cute_triadic_gdn/kernels/sm90_joint_scan.py:1 (the log-space accumulation kernel), MIT; paper-side figures arXiv:2609.36529 App. A via riir-infer .research/007:30. Batch 203 / Plan 203.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: factor every cross-token decay around a MID-CHUNK\n// reference point — 2*C*E exponentials instead of C*C*E. The\n// reference split flips the exponent's sign: a pair entirely BEFORE\n// the reference overflows on `up`, a pair entirely AFTER it overflows\n// on `down` (a straddling pair telescopes safely) — every class exists\n// in a C*C pairwise matrix, so SOME pair overflows whenever mid is\n// strictly inside the chunk. The clamped variant keeps the run alive\n// but no longer telescopes to the true decay (paper-side FP64\n// emulation of exactly this clamp: rel-err 0.05 @ e^-200, 1.5 @ e^-600\n// — arXiv:2609.36529 App. A).\nfn decay_mid_reference_naive(gc: &[f32], r: usize, s: usize, mid: usize) -> f32 {\n    // decay(r <- s) recomposed through the reference\n    let up = gc[r] - gc[mid]; // r before mid: POSITIVE -> exp -> +inf\n    let down = gc[mid] - gc[s]; // s after mid: POSITIVE -> exp -> +inf\n    up.exp() * down.exp() // inf, or inf * 0 = NaN on long-decay spans\n}\n\nfn decay_mid_reference_clamped(gc: &[f32], r: usize, s: usize, mid: usize) -> f32 {\n    let cap = 88.7f32; // the f32 exp overflow knee (ln(f32::MAX) ~ 88.72)\n    let up = (gc[r] - gc[mid]).min(cap);\n    let down = (gc[mid] - gc[s]).min(cap);\n    up.exp() * down.exp() // finite but NOT the decay: the clamp broke\n                          // the telescoping — long-decay factors are lost\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: accumulate LOGS chunk-locally, exponentiate only\n// DIFFERENCES. g <= 0 per token makes gc non-increasing, so every\n// within-chunk r >= s difference is <= 0 BY CONSTRUCTION — exp lands\n// in (0, 1], overflow is impossible, and no clamp exists on the path.\n// The C*C*E exponentials are the price; the mid-reference C*E form is\n// the measured refusal (see ref_shape), not a candidate.\n\n/// chunk-local inclusive cumsum over T of the per-token log gate\n/// (gdn_joint.py `_cumsum`: view(B, T/CHUNK, CHUNK, H, E).cumsum(2);\n/// flattened to one chunk row at a time here).\nfn chunk_cumsum(g: &[f32], chunk: usize) -> Vec<f32> {\n    assert!(chunk > 0 && g.len() % chunk == 0);\n    let mut gc = Vec::with_capacity(g.len());\n    for c in 0..g.len() / chunk {\n        let mut acc = 0.0f32;\n        for t in 0..chunk {\n            acc += g[c * chunk + t]; // the log gate, <= 0 by contract\n            gc.push(acc); // inclusive cumsum: non-increasing in-chunk\n        }\n    }\n    gc\n}\n\n/// decay(r <- s) = exp(gc[r] - gc[s]): the difference is <= 0 whenever\n/// r >= s inside one chunk, so the result is in (0, 1] — finite always.\nfn decay_stable(gc: &[f32], r: usize, s: usize) -> f32 {\n    (gc[r] - gc[s]).exp()\n}\n\n/// the ADMISSIBLE reference form: decays TOWARD the chunk end (state\n/// carry-out, gdn_joint.py:87-89 with gL = gcc[:, -1]) — the end is the\n/// cumsum's minimum, so gc[end] - gc[t] <= 0 for every t <= end: the\n/// bound survives. A MID reference breaks it; the END preserves it.\nfn decay_to_chunk_end(gc: &[f32], t: usize, end: usize) -> f32 {\n    (gc[end] - gc[t]).exp()\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn non_positive_gate_binds_every_argument_by_construction() {\n    // a worst-case log gate: g <= 0 everywhere (the :17 input contract)\n    let g = [-1.5f32, -0.5, -40.0, -0.25, -60.0, -0.125];\n    let chunk = 3usize;\n    let gc = chunk_cumsum(&g, chunk);\n    for c in 0..g.len() / chunk {\n        let base = c * chunk;\n        // the cumsum of non-positive terms is non-increasing...\n        for w in gc[base..base + chunk].windows(2) {\n            assert!(w[1] <= w[0]);\n        }\n        // ...so every r >= s difference is <= 0 and exp lands in (0, 1]\n        for r in base..base + chunk {\n            for s in base..=r {\n                assert!(gc[r] - gc[s] <= 0.0);\n                let d = decay_stable(&gc, r, s);\n                assert!(d.is_finite() && d > 0.0 && d <= 1.0);\n            }\n        }\n        // the end-anchored reference keeps the bound too (end = chunk end)\n        let end = base + chunk - 1;\n        for t in base..=end {\n            let d = decay_to_chunk_end(&gc, t, end);\n            assert!(d.is_finite() && d > 0.0 && d <= 1.0);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn mid_reference_overflows_where_the_stable_form_stays_finite() {\n    // a long-decay chunk at the paper's scale: per-token log gate -100,\n    // g <= 0 throughout. Pair target r=1 <- source s=0, reference mid=2.\n    let g = [0.0f32, -100.0, -100.0, 0.0];\n    let gc = chunk_cumsum(&g, 4);\n    let (r, s, mid) = (1usize, 0usize, 2usize);\n    // stable: exp(gc[r] - gc[s]) = exp(-100) ~ 3.7e-44 — a representable\n    // subnormal: finite, honest, no clamp anywhere on the path\n    let stable = decay_stable(&gc, r, s);\n    assert!(stable.is_finite() && stable > 0.0 && stable < 1e-40);\n    // naive mid-reference: the target sits BEFORE the reference, so the\n    // half-exponent gc[r] - gc[mid] = +100 is POSITIVE — f32 exp is past\n    // its ~e^88.7 knee: +inf; times the other half's underflowed 0.0 the\n    // recomposed decay reads NaN\n    let up = gc[r] - gc[mid];\n    assert!(up > 88.7);\n    assert!(up.exp().is_infinite());\n    assert!((up.exp() * (gc[mid] - gc[s]).exp()).is_nan());\n    // the clamped arm avoids the NaN but stops telescoping: it returns\n    // 0.0 where the true decay is the representable ~3.7e-44 — and the\n    // paper's FP64 emulation of exactly this clamp measures rel-err 0.05\n    // @ within-chunk decay e^-200 and 1.5 @ e^-600 (paper App. A via\n    // riir-infer .research/007:30). The span is lost either way; the\n    // stable form is the one that never needed the patch.\n    let cap = 88.7f32;\n    let clamped = up.min(cap).exp() * (gc[mid] - gc[s]).exp();\n    assert!(clamped.is_finite() && clamped == 0.0);\n    assert!(stable > 0.0); // the truth survives only in the stable form\n}".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
